        "id": "ch8_4_1-3",
        "text": "saving it.\nOpen a new text document using your preferred text editor and save it as key.txt in the same\nfolder as the script \u2013 in my case, c:\\temp\\poshbook\\ch8 . Copy and paste your WeatherApi\nkey into it and save it.\nNow all we need to do is add a parameter that points to the file, and a line that gets the content from\nthe file. Then, we can add that to the $headers variable. It might look something like this:\n[CmdletBinding()]\nParam(\n$City = \"London\",\n$KeyFile = \"key.txt\"\n)\n$key = Get-Content $KeyFile\n$headers = @{\"key\" = \"$key\"}\nWriting Our First Script \u2013 Turning Simple Cmdlets into Reusable Code\n204\nWe\u2019ve identified our changing values and created our parameters.\nActivity 2\nKnowing what we know from previous chapters, how might we rewrite our script so that we\ncan accept multiple values for the -City parameter?\nHint 1: Look at a help file for a cmdlet that accepts multiple strings in a parameter to see how\nwe could write an attribute for the -City parameter. Get-Random accepts multiple objects\nfor -InputObject .\nHint 2: We\u2019re going to want a way of processing each string in turn. Remember, multiple strings\npassed into a parameter are an array.\nHint 3: We\u2019re going to want to send each city to a different output.\nLet\u2019s look at how we might improve them using attributes.",
